[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Big O Notation & Time Complexity Analysis"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"In computer science, Big O notation is used to classify algorithms according to how their run time or space requirements grow as the input size grows. It provides a high-level understanding of the algorithm's efficiency. Time complexity analysis is the process of determining the Big O notation for an algorithm's execution time."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding Big O Notation"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Big O notation expresses the *upper bound* of an algorithm's running time. It describes the worst-case scenario. Common Big O notations include:"},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"O(1): Constant time. The execution time is independent of the input size."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"O(log n): Logarithmic time. The execution time increases logarithmically with the input size. Often seen in binary search."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"O(n): Linear time. The execution time increases linearly with the input size. Example: iterating through an array."},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"O(n log n): Linearithmic time. Often seen in efficient sorting algorithms like merge sort and quicksort (on average)."},{"order":9,"type":"bullet_point","text":"O(n^2): Quadratic time. The execution time increases quadratically with the input size. Example: nested loops iterating through an array."},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"O(2^n): Exponential time. The execution time doubles with each addition to the input data set. Very inefficient."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"O(n!): Factorial time. The execution time grows factorially with the input size. Extremely inefficient."},{"order":12,"type":"subheading","text":"Analyzing Time Complexity"},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"To determine the time complexity of an algorithm, follow these steps:"},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Identify the dominant operations: These are the operations that are executed most frequently as the input size grows."},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Count the number of times the dominant operations are executed as a function of the input size (n)."},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Express the count in terms of Big O notation, ignoring constant factors and lower-order terms."},{"order":17,"type":"subheading","text":"Examples"},{"order":18,"type":"code","text":"// Example 1: O(1) - Constant Time
function getFirstElement(arr) {
  return arr[0];
}"},{"order":19,"type":"code","text":"// Example 2: O(n) - Linear Time
function linearSearch(arr, target) {
  for (let i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {
    if (arr[i] === target) {
      return i;
    }
  }
  return -1;
}"},{"order":20,"type":"code","text":"// Example 3: O(n^2) - Quadratic Time
function bubbleSort(arr) {
  let n = arr.length;
  for (let i = 0; i < n - 1; i++) {
    for (let j = 0; j < n - i - 1; j++) {
      if (arr[j] > arr[j + 1]) {
        // swap arr[j+1] and arr[j]
        let temp = arr[j];
        arr[j] = arr[j + 1];
        arr[j + 1] = temp;
      }
    }
  }
}"},{"order":21,"type":"subheading","text":"Space Complexity"},{"order":22,"type":"paragraph","text":"Space complexity is a measure of the amount of memory space an algorithm requires as a function of the input size. It is also expressed using Big O notation. It considers the space used by variables, data structures, and function call stack."},{"order":23,"type":"paragraph","text":"For example, an algorithm that creates a new array of size 'n' would have a space complexity of O(n)."},{"order":24,"type":"subheading","text":"Importance of Time and Space Complexity"},{"order":25,"type":"paragraph","text":"Understanding time and space complexity is crucial for writing efficient algorithms, especially when dealing with large datasets. Choosing the right algorithm can significantly impact the performance of your application."}]
